Show native views on Wayland: the app's wl_surface as a subsurface below the window, its hole cut by the renderer.

What: `NativeView::new` takes the `wl_proxy` of a role-less `wl_surface` the app made on GPUI's own connection (the
window's display handle), as macOS takes an `NSView`. GPUI gives it a `wp_viewport` at once (and buffer scale 1, the
normal transform) and, the first frame that paints the view, a `wl_subsurface` of the window's surface: synchronized,
an empty input region (all input stays on GPUI's surface: patch 0048's `NativeViewInput::Window`). Each frame, before
the renderer commits the window, a view whose placement changed is synchronized, `set_position` and
`wp_viewport.set_destination` take the element's bounds rounded outwards to whole logical pixels (core's
`native_view_placement::round_outwards`, 0048), and its surface is committed (cached until the window's commit), so
the move, resize, show or hide lands in the frame that moves the hole; once the window has committed, the view goes
back to desynchronized, so the app's own commits show at once and never wake GPUI's frame loop. Whenever the shown
views or their order change, the stack is set again in paint order (`place_below(window)` each, frontmost last). A
view no longer painted gets a null buffer, in step with the window's commit, and keeps its role and subsurface
(Chromium's mutter workaround: mutter may never release a buffer whose surface lost its role); painted again, it is
shown on that kept subsurface. At a fractional scale the hole is cut one physical pixel inside the view (core's
`hole_inset`, applied by patch 0047's `set_native_view_hole_inset`), so the window covers the rounded edges. An opaque
window's opaque region leaves out the shown views. A frame whose draw fails places the views back where the window's
last commit shows them, so a retry that commits the old buffer shows them in step.

A view its element's clip cuts (scrolled partly out of a container) shows only the part the clip leaves, at its full
size and position, as on macOS, iOS and Windows: the subsurface covers that part, and `wp_viewport.set_source` crops
the app's buffer to match. A source past the buffer's edge ends the app's connection, so the app commits every buffer
through `gpui_linux::NativeViewBuffers::of(&view)` (Send, from any thread): `commit_buffer(&surface, &buffer, size)`
and GPUI's placing commits take turns under one lock, each setting the source for the buffer it commits with
(`Committed::NotShown` while the view isn't painted: nothing attached). `NativeView::platform_attachment` (core) is
how `of` reaches the view. A view clipped away entirely hides. In an opaque window whose views ask for a colour
(`NativeView::set_background`, core's `native_view_placement::background`), GPUI's own underlay subsurface, bottom of
the stack, shows it across the views' bounds (a 1×1 `wp_single_pixel_buffer_v1` buffer, else shm, scaled by a
viewport), so a hole never shows the desktop where a view hasn't drawn. Dropping the last `NativeView` handle destroys
the subsurface and viewport. `wl_subcompositor` and `wp_single_pixel_buffer_manager_v1` are bound when offered;
without `wl_subcompositor` or `wp_viewporter`, `NativeView::new` fails as before.

New file `wayland/window/native_view.rs`, ported from chromium/chromium ui/ozone/platform/wayland/host/
wayland_subsurface.cc and wayland_frame_manager.cc @ 4ea2b624ed0c500ec2f23ff17203dff91ad8faa2 (BSD-3-Clause,
Copyright 2020 The Chromium Authors; LICENSE-CHROMIUM, NOTICE, third_party/chromium/PORTED.md, which lists what was
dropped and changed). Table tests: `steps` (placement × frame → step), `clipped_views_show_their_visible_part`,
`frames_stack_views_in_paint_order_over_an_underlay` and `sources_stay_inside_the_buffer` (the crop and the underlay
are new code: Chromium gets its crop rects from its own compositor). `window.rs` holds only the hooks; `client.rs`
binds the globals; `gpui_linux` exports `NativeViewBuffers` and `Committed`. A port, so one patch whatever its size
(patch rule 4).

Why: `NativeView::new` failed on every platform but macOS, so a browser shell (wubble) had no zero-overhead page
view on Linux (xuckless/parallax#1). The window's surface and frame commit are private to gpui_linux.

Upstream PR: not filed yet.

